it is a real honor to be with you today  I used to his Ian just said preach every  Sunday but one of the things that you  discover when you become a Dean and I  was a Dean at Notre Dame is in your  first year you lose your time in your  second year you lose your creativity and  in the third year you lose your mind so  the good news is I'm only in my first  year here the bad news is I was a Dean  for a number of years before I got here  there's a story about a 19th century  minister in Palmyra Missouri named Jacob  Creek Jr who was renowned for the  fervent see with which he prayed and one  particular occasion during the Civil War  his town was surrounded by troops and he  went out as was his custom to a  particular grove to pray he got down on  his knees and just white-headed old man  I've seen a picture of him long hair  full beard just poured his heart out to  God on behalf of his neighbor's his  family his entire town his country and  above all prayed for peace and according  to the story the commander of the troops  was doing reconnaissance and heard  someone speaking rather loudly and crept  up and saw him praying and was so moved  by this prayer that he withdrew his  troops now I can't verify if that  actually happened you may be wondering  well did that really happen that's the  question I asked but whether it happened  or not it teaches us a truth about  ourselves in our relationship with God  and I feel some of the same way about  the text this morning that are the  stories that we've just heard if you ask  me  these things happen precisely as the  third evangelist narrates them I have to  say I don't really know but if you ask  me are they true i will say emphatically  yes and that's what i'd like to think  with you about for a few minutes the  stories begin with Jesus going up onto a  mountain to pray seemed like a very  simple framing device that sets up the  stories until we realize that the third  evangelist routinely marks every major  event of Jesus's life by portraying  Jesus at prayer Jesus is at prayer in  his baptism when the spirit descends  upon him he goes up onto a mountain and  spends an entire night in prayer before  he chooses the 12 he is again at prayer  before he asked the disciples who do  crowd say that I am and then who do you  think I am when Peter made his momentous  confession he is at prayer here before  his glory is revealed he will be at  prayer before he teaches the Our Father  he will again be at prayer in Gethsemane  when he contemplated his immediate  future and he will pray from the cross  why did the third evangelist present  Jesus praying prior to all of these  major events in his life it's a unique  emphasis mark doesn't have it Matthew  doesn't have it the fourth evangelist  doesn't have it why does the third  evangelist emphasize jesus' at prayer  the third evangelist is unique in the  sense that he not only wrote a gospel  and i say he because the personal  pronouns in greek are in the masculine  so it's not an incorrect political slept  just repeating what the text says here  but the third evangelist bright the Acts  of the Apostles as well and thinks of  Christianity as expanding over a wide  horizon of time you don't write the  history of the church if you think the  world is going to end tomorrow and  because of that the third evangelist is  concerned with how do we sustain faith  over our entire lives and so the  Evangelist introduced the parable of the  widow and the judge with these words  then Jesus told them a parable about  their need to pray always and not to  lose heart and concluded it with and yet  when the Son of Man comes will he find  faith on earth faith is sustained  through prayer Jesus has held out as a  paradigm of one who found his own life  with God sustained through his prayer  and through his conversations with God  and so will we but what was the event in  this case what's clearly a revelation of  Jesus in glory the text says his face  change his clothes became dazzlingly  white they saw his glory Moses and  Elijah appeared with him in glory and  then a cloud a symbol of the Divine  Presence overcame the group and  encompassed them but what's the  significance of seeing Jesus in glory  why narrate this the traditional reading  is to say well it's simply an event at  the end of jesus's Galilean ministry one  way to read it others have said Peter  had a vision like Joan of Arc and this  is a record of Peters vision most modern  scholars will say something like  this is actually a post-resurrection  appearance that has been brought up into  the text so that we have a proleptic  understanding of jesus' glory and there  are still others who will say it's a  symbolic way of describing Jesus at his  second coming but however one might  explain the origin of the story it has a  clear function the story is a means of  allowing the disciples and us as those  who hear the story about the disciples  to see the glory of Jesus at a pivotal  moment in his career his ministry in  Galilee is now over he in 951 turns his  face steadfastly to go to Jerusalem  where he's just told his disciples he  will be executed and where Elijah Moses  and Elijah have talked to him about his  departure which is to be fulfilled and  yet for Luke the story is not just about  his death but about his resurrection and  his ascension which this evangelist  narrates this evangelist used at death  as a human verdict leveled against Jesus  that God overturns by the resurrection  in glory so unlike the fourth gospel  which views the lifting up on the cross  as a moment of Jesus's glory this is a  way of saying God overturns the human  verdict by giving Jesus an exalted state  in glory this text allows us understand  who Jesus is before he goes through his  death and that's why it's here the  disciples are rather slow to get this I  don't think they could have gotten into  Yale they struggle with trying to figure  out who Jesus is but the subsequent  story shows them and even poor  alight it is a story of a father meeting  Jesus as he comes down from the mountain  a father who has a son who as the text  says his demon possessed or we would say  is struggling with the neurological  disorder people might say epilepsy maybe  maybe another disorder but the father  had asked the other disciples heal my  son and they couldn't do it and as he  spoke to Jesus his son had what we might  describe as a grand mal seizure and  jesus healed it I can understand the  plight of this father in a very personal  way when my older daughter was a senior  in high school she developed a  neurological disorder it resulted in  events that were similar to but not  identical with seizures one day when she  had one of these attacks in the hospital  she cried out to me and I can see the  scene in my mind very clearly dad make  them stop make them stop and there was  nothing I could do I was doing  everything I could by having her in the  hospital eventually I took her to the  Medical Center in Chicago and they said  that a virus had made its way into her  spinal column and wreak havoc with her  nervous system said it will eventually  dissipate and it did and today she's on  the faculty at the University of  wisconsin-madison very healthy adult for  which I'm very grateful but I however  can never read this story without  identifying with the father when he said  I begged your disciples to cast it out  but they could not now why are these two  stories juxtaposed  why have the story of Jesus in glory  juxtaposed with this poor father who  wants his son healed one of the greatest  Renaissance painters understood this  quite well I think Raphael whose  Transfiguration was commissioned by Pope  Clement the seventh at the time he was  only a cardinal he later became Plymouth  the seven as an altarpiece for their not  bone Cathedral Raphael worked on it off  and on for four years but died before he  completed it but if you've ever seen it  it has two scenes an upper scene and a  lower seen the upper seen is the  Transfiguration the background are  clouds that are luminous with the sun  shining and reflecting on them very  clear the lower half is very dark and  chaotic and i think that the goethe  understood exactly what Raphael was  depicting when he said this about that  painting the two are one below suffering  need above effective power sucker each  bearing on the other both interacting  with one another whether we hear the  stories in the Gospel of Luke or whether  we see the painting of Raphael the point  remains the same we are all reduced to  human frailty at times and forced to ask  to whom  can I turn and the answer of these texts  the answer of Raphael the answer of  Jacob creath is all one in the same we  turn to the risen and glorified Lord
 

